module.exports = [
// Java Notes - deep rewrite (detailed multi-paragraph notes)`r`nmodule.exports = [
  { topic: 'Java Introduction', title: 'Java Fundamentals', content: 'Java is a statically-typed, object-oriented language designed around one promise: write once, run anywhere. Source code compiles to bytecode, an intermediate representation executed by the Java Virtual Machine - so the same class file runs on any JVM, Windows or Linux, with no recompilation. Java is also strongly typed, garbage-collected (no manual memory management) and multi-threaded out of the box, which made it the enterprise and Android workhorse.\n\nThe execution flow to narrate: javac compiles .java sources into .class bytecode; the JVM class loader loads, links (verify, prepare, resolve) and initialises classes; the bytecode interpreter executes, and the JIT compiler hot-spots frequently-run bytecode into optimised native machine code - so Java gets portability AND near-native speed after warmup. JDK versus JRE matters here: the JRE runs programs, the JDK adds development tools (javac, javadoc, jdb).\n\nPlatform editions frame the ecosystem: Java SE (core), Java EE/Jakarta EE (enterprise servers) and Java ME (embedded). Version lineage worth knowing: Java 8 brought lambdas and Streams (the biggest paradigm shift), Java 11 made it LTS with modules maturing, Java 17 and 21 are current LTS releases with records, sealed classes and virtual threads (21). Interview framing: bytecode-and-JVM portability story, JDK vs JRE, then the JDK 8 and 21 highlights.', keyPoints: ['Compile to bytecode; JVM executes it anywhere - WORA', 'javac compiles; class loader loads, links, initialises', 'JIT compiles hot bytecode to native code at runtime', 'JDK = JRE + dev tools (javac, javadoc, jdb)', 'Java 8: lambdas/Streams; 17 and 21: records, sealed, virtual threads'], commonMistakes: ['Saying Java is purely interpreted - the JIT makes it hybrid', 'Confusing JDK and JRE in either direction'], interviewTips: ['Narrate source-to-execution with the JIT story', 'Name Java 8 and Java 21 features to show current knowledge'] },
  { topic: 'JVM JDK JRE', title: 'JVM vs JRE vs JDK', content: 'The three form a containment hierarchy: JVM inside JRE inside JDK. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the abstract computing machine that executes bytecode: it class-loads, verifies, interprets and JIT-compiles, manages the runtime data areas (heap, method area/metaspace, stacks, PC registers, native stacks) and runs the garbage collector. It is what makes bytecode portable - each OS has its own JVM implementation speaking the same class-file format.\n\nThe JRE (Java Runtime Environment) = JVM plus the core class libraries (java.lang, java.util, java.io) - everything needed to RUN an application, nothing to develop one. The JDK (Java Development Kit) = JRE plus development tools: javac the compiler, javadoc the documentation generator, jar the packager, jdb the debugger, plus diagnostics like jstack, jmap, jconsole. Since JDK 11 Oracle ships only JDKs (no standalone JRE), so modern installs are all JDK.\n\nThe interview depth beyond definitions: JIT compilation (HotSpot profiles hot methods, compiles them to native with inlining and escape analysis), class loading (bootstrap, platform, application loaders; delegation model), and the memory areas (per-thread stacks vs shared heap). Saying JVM executes bytecode is the 10-second answer; explaining JIT plus class loading plus memory areas is the senior answer. Interview framing: containment, then JVM internals, then JDK tools.', keyPoints: ['JVM executes bytecode; JRE = JVM + libraries; JDK = JRE + tools', 'JVM areas: heap, metaspace/method area, stacks, PC, native', 'JIT (HotSpot) compiles hot paths to optimised native code', 'Class loaders delegate: bootstrap -> platform -> application', 'JDK tools: javac, jar, javadoc, jdb, jmap, jstack'], commonMistakes: ['Saying the JRE contains the JDK - it is the reverse', 'Claiming one compiled class needs recompilation per OS'], interviewTips: ['Draw the JDK > JRE > JVM containment, then go internal', 'Explain JIT with inlining and hot-path profiling'] },
  { topic: 'Data Types and Variables', title: 'Data Types and Variables', content: 'Java is statically and strongly typed: every variable declares its type at compile time. Primitives are the eight value types: byte (8-bit), short (16), int (32), long (64), float (32), double (64), char (16-bit unsigned UTF-16 code unit) and boolean - primitives hold VALUES directly on the stack (for locals) and have no methods. Everything else is a reference type: objects live on the heap, variables hold references to them, and they inherit from Object with methods and possible null.\n\nThe consequences interviewers probe: primitives use == for value comparison while references compare IDENTITY with == and must use equals() for value equality; primitives cannot be null (an int is always 0) but wrappers can (Integer i = null throws NPE on unboxing); and numeric promotion rules (byte + byte = int, integer division truncates, floating point is IEEE 754 - never use == for money, use BigDecimal). Autoboxing converts int <-> Integer seamlessly but with a cost: boxing allocates, and == on boxed types compares references (with the Integer cache gotcha: -128..127 are cached so 127 == 127 is true but 128 == 128 is false for boxed values).\n\nVariables: local (stack, must be initialised before use), instance fields (per-object on heap, get defaults: 0, false, null), static fields (per-class, shared). final makes constants or unassignable references; var (Java 10) is local type inference only - the type is still static. Interview framing: the eight primitives, primitive-vs-reference memory and equality, then autoboxing with the Integer cache trap.', keyPoints: ['Eight primitives hold values; references point to heap objects', '== on primitives compares values, on references identity', 'Wrappers can be null; unboxing null throws NPE', 'Integer cache: -128..127 boxed values are interned', 'Defaults: fields get 0/false/null; locals must be set'], commonMistakes: ['Comparing boxed Integers with == beyond the cache range', 'Using float/double for currency - use BigDecimal'], interviewTips: ['Demo the 127 vs 128 boxed equality trap', 'Explain stack vs heap placement for locals vs fields'] },
  { topic: 'Operators', title: 'Operators in Java', content: 'Java operator families: arithmetic (+ - * / %), assignment (= plus compound += -= etc.), comparison (== != < > <= >=), logical (&& || with short-circuit, & | without), bitwise (& | ^ ~ << >> >>>), unary (++ -- ! + -), ternary (cond ? a : b) and instanceof. The must-explain behaviours: integer division truncates toward zero (7/2 = 3), % keeps the dividend sign in Java (-7 % 2 = -1), and ++i versus i++ (pre vs post increment value).\n\nShort-circuit evaluation is the interview favourite: && stops evaluating if the left operand is false, || stops if true - which makes null guards like if (s != null && s.length() > 0) safe, and enables deliberate guard sequences. The non-short-circuit & and | evaluate BOTH sides always (and on booleans act logically, on ints bitwise). >>> is the unsigned right shift (zero-fills) while >> sign-extends - a favourite trivia question. Operator precedence: * / % bind tighter than + -, comparisons tighter than && which binds tighter than ||, assignment lowest - parenthesise anything non-obvious.\n\nThe equality subtlety returns here: == on primitives compares values, on objects identity; string literals are interned so "abc" == "abc" is true but new String("abc") == "abc" is false - always compare strings with equals(). Interview framing: families, short-circuit with the null-guard pattern, >>> vs >>, then the == vs equals string trap.', keyPoints: ['Integer division truncates; % keeps dividend sign', '&& and || short-circuit; & and | evaluate both sides', '>>> zero-fills right shift; >> sign-extends', 'Precedence: arithmetic > comparison > && > || > assignment', '== compares identity for objects - use equals()'], commonMistakes: ['Using == on Strings because literals happen to be interned', 'Forgetting ++i vs i++ semantics in loop expressions'], interviewTips: ['Show the null-safe short-circuit guard idiom', 'Explain >>> vs >> with a negative number example'] },
  { topic: 'Control Flow', title: 'Control Flow Statements', content: 'Decision statements: if / else if / else, switch, and the ternary operator. The classic switch trap is fall-through - without break, execution continues into the next case; the modern arrow syntax (Java 14+) removes the hazard: switch (day) { case MONDAY -> ...; } also yields values (switch expressions) and forces exhaustiveness when switching over enums. Conditions require boolean strictly - while (1) is a C idiom that does NOT compile in Java.\n\nLoops: for (init; cond; update) when the count is known, enhanced for (for-each: for (String s : list)) over arrays and Iterables, while (pre-test) and do-while (post-test, runs at least once). Jump statements: break exits the loop, continue skips to the next iteration, and labelled break/continue (outer: for ... break outer;) exits nested loops - cleaner than flags. return exits methods.\n\nDetails worth raising: the for-each cannot modify the underlying collection (ConcurrentModificationException on remove - use Iterator.remove or removeIf), cannot index (no access to position), and works on arrays and anything Iterable. Infinite loop idioms: while (true) with break inside. Interview framing: if/switch with the fall-through and arrow-syntax points, loop selection rules, then the for-each limitation with the CME story.', keyPoints: ['switch falls through without break; arrow syntax fixes it', 'Switch expressions (14+) return values and enforce exhaustiveness', 'for / for-each / while / do-while each fit different shapes', 'Labelled break exits nested loops cleanly', 'for-each cannot structurally modify the collection (CME)'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting break in a classic switch and falling through', 'Removing items inside a for-each and hitting CME'], interviewTips: ['Explain fall-through with a deliberate-use example', 'Give Iterator.remove or removeIf as the CME fix'] },
  { topic: 'Arrays', title: 'Arrays in Java', content: 'An array is a fixed-length, homogeneous, indexed container allocated on the heap: int[] a = new int[5] creates five zero-initialised slots; literals use int[] a = {1, 2, 3}. Length is a final FIELD (a.length - no parentheses, unlike Strings length() method), indexing is O(1) with bounds checking - out of range throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException at runtime. Arrays are covariant (String[] is a subtype of Object[]), which is why ArrayStoreException exists - storing an Integer in an Object[] that is really a String[] compiles but fails at runtime; generics avoid this.\n\nMulti-dimensional arrays are arrays of arrays: int[][] grid = new int[3][4] gives three int[4] references (jagged arrays allowed: new int[3][] with varying row lengths). Utility workhorses in java.util.Arrays: sort (dual-pivot quicksort for primitives, TimSort for objects), binarySearch (sorted arrays only), fill, copyOf, copyOfRange, equals, deepEquals, asList (fixed-size view backed by the array). System.arraycopy is the native fast copy.\n\nArrays vs ArrayList is the standard follow-up: arrays are fixed-size, can hold primitives efficiently, slightly faster; ArrayList is resizable, only objects (boxing for primitives), rich API. Arrays.asList returns a fixed-size list backed by the array - add/remove throws UnsupportedOperationException, a classic gotcha. Interview framing: declaration and length field, bounds checking and covariance exception, multi-dimensional/jagged, then Arrays utilities and the asList trap.', keyPoints: ['Fixed size; .length is a field, O(1) indexed access', 'Bounds-checked: AIOOBE at runtime', 'Jagged multi-dim: array of arrays', 'Arrays.sort: quicksort for primitives, TimSort for objects', 'Arrays.asList: fixed-size view - no add/remove'], commonMistakes: ['Calling a.length() instead of a.length', 'Mutating the list from Arrays.asList and hitting UnsupportedOperationException'], interviewTips: ['Explain covariance with ArrayStoreException', 'Contrast arrays vs ArrayList with the boxing cost'] },
  { topic: 'Strings', title: 'Strings in Java', content: 'String is an immutable reference type: every mutation creates a new object. Immutability buys thread safety, safe sharing, and enables the string pool - the JVM interns literals so identical literals share one heap object, making "abc" == "abc" true while new String("abc") == "abc" is false (it forces a new object). Compare strings with equals() for content, equalsIgnoreCase for case-insensitive, compareTo for ordering; == compares references only.\n\nThe API surface interviewers exercise: length(), charAt, substring (start inclusive, end exclusive), indexOf/lastIndexOf, contains, replace, split (regex - escape metacharacters like the dot), trim/strip, toUpperCase/toLowerCase, join, chars, format, valueOf. String concatenation in a loop is the performance classic: each + creates a new String (and since Java 9 compiles to invokedynamic makeConcat, still allocating); use StringBuilder (non-thread-safe, fast) in loops, StringBuffer only when genuinely shared across threads (synchronized methods, slower).\n\nImmutability internals worth naming: String caches its hashCode (computed once, why String is a great HashMap key), and the pool lives in the heap since Java 7 (previously permgen); intern() moves a string into the pool. Switch on strings works (hash-based). Interview framing: immutability and why, pool plus the == trap, key API methods, then StringBuilder in loops with the hash-caching point.', keyPoints: ['Immutable: mutations allocate new Strings', 'String pool interns literals; == compares references', 'equals() for content; compareTo for ordering', 'StringBuilder in loops; StringBuffer only for shared threads', 'Cached hashCode makes String an ideal HashMap key'], commonMistakes: ['Building strings with + inside loops - quadratic allocations', 'Comparing new String("x") with == and expecting true'], interviewTips: ['Explain why immutability enables pooling and hash caching', 'Walk the concat-in-loop vs StringBuilder benchmark reasoning'] },
  { topic: 'OOP Concepts', title: 'OOP Concepts in Java', content: 'The four pillars: ENCAPSULATION - bundle data and methods, hide state behind private fields with controlled accessors, so invariants cannot be bypassed; INHERITANCE - classes extend classes (single) and implement interfaces (multiple), reusing and specialising behaviour (is-a); POLYMORPHISM - one interface, many implementations: method overriding is runtime polymorphism resolved by the actual object type (dynamic dispatch through the vtable), overloading is compile-time resolved by signature; ABSTRACTION - expose what, hide how, via abstract classes and interfaces.\n\nSupporting concepts: classes are blueprints, objects are instances; constructors initialise (overloadable, chainable via this() and super()); static members belong to the class not instances; composition (has-a fields) is preferred over inheritance for reuse because it avoids fragile base class coupling - the principle favour composition over inheritance. The Liskov substitution principle governs sound overriding: a subclass must be usable anywhere the parent is.\n\nThe dynamic dispatch mechanics deserve precision: Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak() invokes Dogs speak because dispatch is on the RUNTIME type while field access and static methods bind on the COMPILE-TIME type - that asymmetry is a beloved interview trap. Java is not purely object-oriented: primitives and static exist. Interview framing: name the four pillars with one crisp purpose each, single inheritance + multiple interface implementation, runtime vs compile-time polymorphism, then the dispatch trap.', keyPoints: ['Encapsulation: private state + accessors protect invariants', 'Inheritance: single class extension, multiple interfaces', 'Runtime polymorphism = overriding via dynamic dispatch', 'Overloading resolved at compile time by signature', 'Favour composition over inheritance (fragile base problem)'], commonMistakes: ['Calling overloading runtime polymorphism - it is compile-time', 'Saying Java is 100 percent OOP - primitives and static exist'], interviewTips: ['Explain the Animal/Dog dispatch and field-access asymmetry', 'Argue composition-first with the fragile-base-class reason'] },
  { topic: 'Encapsulation', title: 'Encapsulation', content: 'Encapsulation hides an objects internal state and exposes behaviour through a controlled interface: private fields, public methods. Access modifiers rank visibility: private (class only), default/package-private (no modifier: same package), protected (package + subclasses), public (everywhere). The classic pattern: private fields with public getters/setters, where the SETTER is the enforcement point - it can validate (setAge rejects negatives), maintain invariants (keep start <= end), fire events or mark dirty flags. Fields stay private even with getters because the INTERNAL REPRESENTATION stays free to change.\n\nWhy it matters beyond style: without encapsulation, every caller that touches a field directly becomes a dependency on the representation - changing int count to AtomicInteger or adding caching breaks them all. With encapsulation, invariants hold no matter who calls: a Money class can never exist with a negative amount if the only entry points validate. Immutable classes are encapsulation perfected: final fields, no setters, defensive copies of mutable inputs/outputs (Date fields must be copied both in and out - the classic leak).\n\nJavaBeans conventions (getX/setX) and records (Java 16: compact immutable data carriers with final components and auto-generated accessors, equals, hashCode, toString) frame the modern answer: records give you encapsulated immutability for free for data classes. Interview framing: definition, modifier ladder, setter-as-guard with a validation example, then immutability and defensive copies.', keyPoints: ['private fields + controlled public methods', 'Modifiers: private < package < protected < public', 'Setters enforce validation and invariants', 'Representation can change without breaking callers', 'Immutable + defensive copies = encapsulation perfected'], commonMistakes: ['Exposing mutable internal collections or Date fields directly', 'Declaring everything public for convenience'], interviewTips: ['Show one invariant that only a setter can protect', 'Mention records as modern immutable data carriers'] },
  { topic: 'Inheritance', title: 'Inheritance in Java', content: 'Inheritance models is-a specialisation: class Dog extends Animal inherits members and can add or override behaviour. Java allows SINGLE class inheritance (one direct superclass - avoids the diamond problem of C++ multiple inheritance) but MULTIPLE interface implementation, and every class implicitly extends Object. The super keyword reaches the parent: super() calls the parent constructor (must be first statement; if omitted the compiler inserts a no-arg call - which is why a parent without a no-arg constructor breaks subclasses), super.method() reaches overridden implementations.\n\nWhat is and is not inherited: public/protected fields and methods are; constructors are NOT (they are invoked, not inherited); private members are not accessible (though they exist in the object); static members are shared and hidden, not overridden. Method OVERRIDING requires the same signature and compatible return (covariant returns allowed); the @Override annotation makes the compiler verify. Overridden methods can widen visibility (protected -> public) but not narrow; they cannot throw broader checked exceptions. final classes (String) cannot be extended; final methods cannot be overridden; private and static methods are not polymorphic (bound statically - static hiding resolves by reference type).\n\nDesign guidance: inheritance couples child to parent implementation details (fragile base class problem) - use it for genuine is-a with substitutability (Liskov), otherwise compose. Interview framing: single + interface-multiple, super and constructor chaining, the inherited/not-inherited list, then the fragile-base warning.', keyPoints: ['Single class inheritance, multiple interfaces, root Object', 'super() chains constructors; must be first statement', 'Constructors and private members are not inherited', 'Overriding: same signature, covariant return, no narrowing', 'final class/method blocks extension and overriding'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting the implicit super() and hitting no-arg constructor errors', 'Expecting static method hiding to be dynamic'], interviewTips: ['State the not-inherited list precisely', 'Explain the fragile base class problem to justify composition'] },
  { topic: 'Polymorphism', title: 'Polymorphism', content: 'Polymorphism - one interface, many behaviours - comes in two Java forms. Compile-time (static): method OVERLOADING - same name, different parameter lists; the compiler picks the signature at compile time. Runtime (dynamic): method OVERRIDING - a subclass redefines a superclass method with the same signature, and the JVM dispatches on the ACTUAL object type at runtime via the vtable - this is why Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak() prints the Dog version. Runtime polymorphism is what interviews mean by default.\n\nThe overriding rules to recite: same name and parameter list; covariant return types allowed; cannot reduce visibility (public cannot become protected); cannot throw broader checked exceptions; static methods are HIDDEN not overridden (no dynamic dispatch); instance variables are not polymorphic (field access resolves on the declared type); final and private methods cannot be overridden. The @Override annotation catches signature typos at compile time - always use it.\n\nDesign relevance: runtime polymorphism powers dependency injection, the Strategy pattern and test doubles - code against the interface and swap implementations. The overload-vs-override traps (static hiding, field hiding) are the favourite trick questions. Interview framing: define both forms with examples, recite the overriding rules, then demonstrate the static/field hiding traps.', keyPoints: ['Overloading = compile-time, decided by argument types', 'Overriding = runtime dispatch on the actual object type', 'Covariant returns OK; visibility cannot shrink; no broader checked exceptions', 'static methods hide, never override - no polymorphism', 'Fields are not polymorphic - resolved by declared type'], commonMistakes: ['Calling overloading runtime polymorphism', 'Expecting polymorphic behaviour from instance fields'], interviewTips: ['Write the Animal/Dog override example and predict the output', 'Explain how @Override catches a silent overload bug'] },
  { topic: 'Abstraction and Interfaces', title: 'Abstraction and Interfaces', content: 'Abstraction exposes WHAT an object does while hiding HOW - achieved through abstract classes and interfaces. An abstract class cannot be instantiated, may mix abstract methods (no body) with concrete state and behaviour, has constructors and any access modifiers, and models an IS-A relationship sharing code. An interface declares a contract: since Java 8 it may include default and static methods, and since Java 9 private helpers; its fields are implicitly public static final (constants only), and it has no constructors or instance state.\n\nThe when-to-use answer: abstract class when related classes share code and state (Template Method pattern); interface when unrelated classes must honour one capability (Comparable, Serializable, Runnable) - interface = CAN-DO contract, abstract class = IS-A with shared plumbing. A class extends exactly one class but implements many interfaces - multiple inheritance of TYPE without the diamond problem, because interfaces carry no state; conflicting default methods must be resolved explicitly with InterfaceName.super.method().\n\nThe trick-question set: interface variables cannot be private or non-final; abstract classes CAN have constructors and main(); an abstract class need not contain any abstract method; default methods enabled library evolution (Collection.stream() added in Java 8 without breaking every existing implementation). Interview framing: define both mechanisms, state the IS-A vs CAN-DO choice, then the Java 8 evolution story.', keyPoints: ['abstract class: partial abstraction, state + constructors + mixed methods', 'interface: contract; Java 8 default/static, Java 9 private methods', 'Interface fields: public static final only', 'One superclass, many interfaces - diamond solved via super-resolution', 'Abstract for shared code (IS-A); interface for capability (CAN-DO)'], commonMistakes: ['Saying interfaces can hold instance state', 'Claiming an abstract class must have abstract methods'], interviewTips: ['Give Template Method vs Comparable as the choosing examples', 'Explain how default methods avoided breaking collections'] },
  { topic: 'Exception Handling', title: 'Exception Handling', content: 'Java exceptions are objects descending from Throwable: Error (JVM-fatal: OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError - do not catch) and Exception. Checked exceptions (IOException, SQLException) extend Exception but NOT RuntimeException - the compiler FORCES catch or throws declaration, for recoverable external failures; unchecked (NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBounds, IllegalArgumentException) extend RuntimeException - programming bugs, no compile-time obligation. This checked/unchecked split and its rationale is the core answer.\n\nMechanics: try/catch/finally - finally ALWAYS runs (even after a return in try; only System.exit skips it), used for resource release; try-with-resources (Java 7+) auto-closes any AutoCloseable, calling close() in reverse order and reporting suppressed exceptions - the modern replacement for finally-close blocks. Multi-catch catch (IOException | SQLException e) reduces duplication. throw raises, throws declares. Exceptions cost when THROWN (stack trace filling), not when handled - never use them for control flow in hot paths.\n\nCraft rules that mark seniority: catch specific types, never swallow (empty catch), never catch Throwable/Error, wrap and rethrow with context (new ServiceException(msg, e)) preserving the cause chain, create custom exceptions extending RuntimeException for domain failures, and log-OR-rethrow not both blindly. A return inside finally swallows the in-flight exception - the classic trap. Interview framing: hierarchy and the checked rationale, finally semantics and try-with-resources, then the craft rules.', keyPoints: ['Throwable -> Error (fatal) and Exception', 'Checked = compiler-forced recovery; unchecked = bugs', 'finally always runs; try-with-resources auto-closes AutoCloseable', 'Multi-catch with |; the cost is in throwing, not catching', 'Wrap with context, preserve the cause, never swallow'], commonMistakes: ['Catching Exception and continuing silently', 'Returning from finally and hiding the in-flight exception'], interviewTips: ['Explain WHY IOException is checked but NPE is not', 'Show a try-with-resources block and the suppressed-exception story'] },
  { topic: 'Collections Framework', title: 'Collections Framework', content: 'The Java Collections Framework is a hierarchy of interfaces with concrete implementations: Iterable -> Collection -> List, Set, Queue (Map sits beside, not under, Collection). LIST is ordered with duplicates allowed: ArrayList (dynamic array - O(1) random access, amortised O(1) append, O(n) middle insert/delete, default capacity 10 growing 1.5x) versus LinkedList (doubly-linked - O(1) insert at ends, O(n) access, also a Deque, but loses to ArrayDeque for queue duty). SET rejects duplicates: HashSet (HashMap-backed, O(1) average, no order), LinkedHashSet (insertion order via internal linked list), TreeSet (Red-Black tree, sorted O(log n), NavigableSet ceiling/floor). QUEUE: PriorityQueue (binary heap, O(log n) offer/poll, NOT FIFO - orders by comparator) and Deque/ArrayDeque (the fastest stack and queue implementation).\n\nMAP is key-value with its own tree: HashMap (O(1) average, one null key, unordered; buckets + chained/red-black bins since Java 8; treeified when a bin exceeds 8 entries if the table is large), LinkedHashMap (insertion or access order - the base for LRU caches via removeEldestEntry), TreeMap (sorted by key, O(log n), NavigableMap), Hashtable (legacy, synchronized - replaced by ConcurrentHashMap). Synchronisation story: Vector and Hashtable are legacy; Collections.synchronizedList wraps with coarse locking; java.util.concurrent gives ConcurrentHashMap (lock striping / CAS bins) and CopyOnWriteArrayList (snapshot iteration for read-mostly).\n\nThe interview probe set: HashMap working (hashCode -> bucket -> equals within bucket), fail-fast iterators (ConcurrentModificationException via modCount - use Iterator.remove or concurrent collections), ArrayList vs LinkedList choice (almost always ArrayList - cache locality), Arrays.asList fixed-size vs ArrayList, and equals/hashCode contract violations silently breaking HashSets. Interview framing: draw the interface tree, compare implementations by operation complexity, then the HashMap internals story.', keyPoints: ['List ordered with dupes; Set unique; Queue FIFO/priority; Map key-value', 'ArrayList O(1) access vs LinkedList O(n) - prefer ArrayList', 'HashSet O(1) unordered; LinkedHashSet insertion order; TreeSet sorted', 'HashMap: buckets, treeified bins; TreeMap sorted; LinkedHashMap access order', 'Concurrent: ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList; legacy Vector/Hashtable'], commonMistakes: ['Believing LinkedList beats ArrayList for inserts (needs O(n) traversal first)', 'Removing while iterating without Iterator.remove - CME'], interviewTips: ['Draw the Collection hierarchy from memory', 'State O() complexity for each headline implementation'] },
  { topic: 'ArrayList vs LinkedList', title: 'ArrayList vs LinkedList', content: 'ArrayList is a resizable array: get(i) is O(1) direct indexing, add at the end is amortised O(1) (occasional O(n) growth - new array 1.5x and copy), insert/delete in the middle is O(n) because elements shift, and memory is compact and cache-friendly. LinkedList is a doubly-linked list: add/remove at the HEAD or via an existing iterator node is O(1), but get(i) is O(n) traversal from the nearest end, insert in the middle is O(n) to FIND the position, and every node pays two references plus object-header overhead with pointer-chasing that destroys CPU cache locality.\n\nThe myth interviewers love correcting: people claim LinkedList is better at insertion - but inserting into the MIDDLE still costs O(n) to reach the position, and in benchmarks ArrayList wins in nearly all realistic patterns because of cache locality and cheap bulk System.arraycopy shifts. LinkedList only shines with O(1) head/tail mutations used as a Deque - and even there ArrayDeque beats it. Default growth: new ArrayList() starts at capacity 10; specifying capacity upfront (new ArrayList<>(expectedSize)) avoids repeated grow-copies - a standard performance tip.\n\nMemory arithmetic to quote: a LinkedList of a million Integers costs roughly 40+ bytes per node (header + item + prev + next) against 4-8 bytes per slot in the ArrayList backing array - an order of magnitude. Interview framing: complexity table row by row, the cache-locality argument, the Deque exception, then capacity planning.', keyPoints: ['ArrayList: O(1) get, amortised append, O(n) middle ops, cache-friendly', 'LinkedList: O(1) at ends/iterator, O(n) access and search', 'Middle insertion still O(n) for LinkedList - the common misconception', 'ArrayDeque beats LinkedList as queue/stack', 'Pre-size large ArrayLists to avoid grow-copies'], commonMistakes: ['Choosing LinkedList for random access patterns', 'Forgetting iterator overhead negates theoretical O(1) node insertion'], interviewTips: ['Recite the complexity table both directions', 'Explain cache locality as the real-world decider'] },
  { topic: 'HashMap', title: 'HashMap Internals', content: 'HashMap stores key-value pairs in an array of buckets: put() computes hashCode() of the key, spreads the high bits (h ^ (h >>> 16)) to reduce clustering, then indexes bucket = (n - 1) & hash (power-of-two table size makes this a cheap mod). Collisions chain entries in the bucket; since Java 8, a bucket with more than TREEIFY_THRESHOLD (8) entries and a table of at least MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY (64) converts the chain to a RED-BLACK TREE, making worst-case lookup O(log n) instead of O(n) - the flagship internal to narrate. get() repeats the hash, walks the bucket comparing hashes then equals().\n\nThe contract is everything: equal objects MUST have equal hash codes. Override equals() without hashCode() and two equal keys may land in different buckets - get() returns null for a key you just put; the reverse (same hash for unequal objects) is merely collisions, handled by chaining. Resizing: when size exceeds capacity * loadFactor (default 0.75), the table doubles and every entry rehashes - O(n) per resize, amortised O(1) per put; pre-size large maps to avoid it. Nulls: one null key allowed (stored in bucket 0), many null values.\n\nJava 8+ details worth naming: treeified bins use compareTo/tiebreak when keys share hashCodes, iteration order is unspecified (LinkedHashMap for insertion/access order, TreeMap for sorted), and HashMap is not thread-safe - Java 7 concurrent resize could loop forever; use ConcurrentHashMap. Interview framing: hash-to-bucket flow with the bit operations, treeification, equals/hashCode contract with the broken-map example, then resizing and concurrency.', keyPoints: ['bucket = (n-1) & hash with bit-spread; power-of-two capacity', 'Collisions chain; >8 entries treeify to red-black O(log n)', 'equals/hashCode contract: equal keys must hash equal', 'Resize doubles at loadFactor 0.75 - amortised O(1) put', 'One null key; no ordering; not thread-safe - use CHM'], commonMistakes: ['Overriding equals without hashCode and losing entries', 'Assuming any iteration order - it is explicitly unspecified'], interviewTips: ['Walk put() end to end including treeification', 'Demonstrate the equals-without-hashCode bug live'] },
  { topic: 'Generics', title: 'Generics', content: 'Generics parameterise types for compile-time safety: List<String> rejects non-Strings at compile time, eliminating casts and ClassCastException risk - the pre-Java-5 Object-list world required manual casts and failed at runtime. Type parameters appear on classes (class Box<T>), interfaces and methods (static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> xs)); the compiler type-checks then ERASES: at runtime T is replaced by its bound (or Object), with casts inserted at call sites - so generics exist for the compiler only, and List<String>.class == List<Integer>.class.\n\nConsequences of erasure to state precisely: no primitive type arguments (List<int> illegal - use Integer and pay boxing), no generic instance creation new T(), no generic arrays new T[], no instanceof List<String>, and no overloads differing only in generic parameters. Bounds: <T extends Number> allows calling Number methods on T; multiple bounds <T extends A & B>; recursive bounds <T extends Comparable<T>> are the standard max/sort signature.\n\nWildcards are the interview deep end: List<Object> is NOT a supertype of List<String> (invariance) - that is why ? extends and ? super exist. PECS: Producer Extends - a source you read FROM takes ? extends T (List<? extends Number> accepts List<Integer>, reads yield Number); Consumer Super - a sink you write INTO takes ? super T (List<? super Integer> accepts List<Object>, writes Integers, reads yield Object). Unbounded <?> for read-only unknowns. Arrays are covariant and reified (ArrayStoreException possible at runtime) while generics are invariant and erased - that contrast explains why new T[] is illegal. Interview framing: the erasure story, bounds, then PECS with a copy(src, dst) signature.', keyPoints: ['Compile-time type safety; erasure removes generics at runtime', 'No new T(), T[], primitives, or instanceof List<String>', 'extends bounds; invariance: List<Object> is not a supertype of List<String>', 'PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super', 'Arrays covariant + reified vs generics invariant + erased'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting runtime type info from erased generics', 'Using wildcards in return types (keep APIs simple)'], interviewTips: ['Explain erasure with List.class equality', 'Apply PECS to a copy(src, dst) signature live'] },
  { topic: 'Multithreading', title: 'Multithreading', content: 'A Java thread is an independent execution path sharing its process heap - cheaper context switches than processes, but shared memory demands coordination. Creating threads: extends Thread, implements Runnable (preferred - keeps inheritance free and works with executors), or Callable<V> with Future for value-returning tasks. Lifecycle: NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED (waiting on a monitor lock), WAITING (wait()/join() indefinitely), TIMED_WAITING (sleep(n), wait(n)), TERMINATED. Never start a thread twice (IllegalThreadStateException) and never call run() directly - that just executes on the current thread.\n\nThe modern reality: threads are expensive (about 1MB stack each, OS-scheduled), so raw new Thread() gave way to EXECUTORS - ThreadPoolExecutor with core/max pool sizes, a work queue and a rejection policy; Executors.newFixedThreadPool, newCachedThreadPool, newSingleThreadExecutor, and ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor for delayed/periodic work. Always submit tasks, never manage threads; prefer the explicit ThreadPoolExecutor constructor in production because the convenience factories have documented footguns (unbounded queue in fixed pools, unbounded thread growth in cached). Java 21 virtual threads (Project Loom) make million-thread server styles practical.\n\nCore coordination tools: synchronized and monitors for mutual exclusion, wait/notify on the monitor, volatile for visibility, Lock/Condition from java.util.concurrent.locks, semaphores, CountDownLatch/CyclicBarrier/Phaser for rendezvous, and BlockingQueue for producer-consumer without hand-rolled wait logic. Interview framing: Runnable vs Thread, the six lifecycle states, thread-creation cost driving executors, then the coordination toolbox.', keyPoints: ['Threads share heap; Runnable preferred over extends Thread', 'Six states: NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING, TERMINATED', 'run() directly runs on the caller thread - use start()', 'Executors: fixed/cached/scheduled; prefer explicit ThreadPoolExecutor', 'Coordination: monitors, Lock/Condition, latch/barrier, BlockingQueue'], commonMistakes: ['Calling run() instead of start()', 'Creating unbounded thread counts per request - OOM and thrashing'], interviewTips: ['Recite the six states with a triggering example each', 'Explain why executors replaced manual thread creation'] },
  { topic: 'Thread Lifecycle', title: 'Thread Lifecycle and States', content: 'A Java thread moves through six states (java.lang.Thread.State). NEW: created, start() not called. RUNNABLE: eligible to run - either executing on a core or ready in the OS run queue (Java merges ready + running into one state). BLOCKED: waiting to acquire a monitor lock held by another thread (synchronized entry). WAITING: parked indefinitely via Object.wait() with no timeout, Thread.join(), or LockSupport.park() - released only when another thread notifies or joins back. TIMED_WAITING: the same parking with a timeout - sleep(ms), wait(ms), join(ms), parkNanos. TERMINATED: run() completed or an exception escaped.\n\nThe transitions to narrate precisely: start() moves NEW -> RUNNABLE (one-way - a terminated thread cannot restart; calling start() again throws IllegalThreadStateException). Entering a synchronized block of a contended monitor moves a thread to BLOCKED; when the monitor frees, the OS re-arms it back to RUNNABLE. Calling obj.wait() inside the monitor RELEASES the lock (favourite trick: sleep() does NOT release locks, wait() does) and moves to WAITING; notify() moves the waiter to BLOCKED (it must re-acquire the monitor before returning from wait()).\n\nThe sleep-vs-wait table: sleep is a Thread static, keeps all locks, usable anywhere; wait is an Object method, releases the monitor, only legal inside synchronized, pairs with notify/notifyAll. Also name join() (caller waits for completion) and interrupt() (sets the flag; blocking methods throw InterruptedException and CLEAR it - handle, do not swallow). Interview framing: six states with a trigger each, the BLOCKED/WAITING distinction, then the sleep-vs-wait contract.', keyPoints: ['Six states: NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING, TERMINATED', 'start() is one-way; restart throws IllegalThreadStateException', 'wait() releases the monitor; sleep() keeps all locks', 'notify() wakes a waiter into BLOCKED - it must re-acquire first', 'interrupt() sets a flag; blocking calls throw and clear it'], commonMistakes: ['Saying RUNNABLE means currently executing - it includes ready-queued', 'Calling wait() outside synchronized - IllegalMonitorStateException'], interviewTips: ['Draw the state diagram with the method on each edge', 'Contrast sleep, wait, join, park in one table'] },
  { topic: 'Synchronization', title: 'Synchronization in Java', content: 'Java synchronisation guards shared mutable state against race conditions and visibility failures. The intrinsic monitor: every object has a lock; synchronized method locks this, synchronized(obj) locks obj; a thread entering acquires, others BLOCK. It is reentrant - the owner may re-acquire (recursion safe). Memory semantics: unlock happens-before subsequent lock, so synchronized fixes BOTH atomicity and visibility (a plain boolean flag without volatile can loop forever in a reader thread due to caching - the classic demonstration).\n\nThe tools beyond synchronized: volatile for visibility-only flags (no atomicity - count++ still races); AtomicBoolean/AtomicInteger/AtomicLong/AtomicReference with hardware CAS for lock-free counters and compare-and-set loops; ReentrantLock (explicit lock/unlock in finally, tryLock with timeout, fair mode, multiple Conditions) when monitors are not enough; ReadWriteLock/StampedLock for read-mostly data; and the coordination builders - Semaphore (permits), CountDownLatch (one-shot gate), CyclicBarrier (reusable rendezvous), Phaser (flexible), Exchanger.\n\nProduction practice: prefer immutable objects and thread-confinement first, then concurrent collections (ConcurrentHashMap instead of synchronizedMap, BlockingQueue instead of hand-rolled wait/notify pipelines), then executors. Name the classics when asked for bugs: check-then-act races (if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v) - use putIfAbsent), lost updates on counters, and double-checked locking requiring the volatile keyword. Interview framing: monitor semantics + happens-before, then the volatile vs atomic vs lock ladder, then the classic bugs.', keyPoints: ['Every object has a reentrant monitor; synchronized acquires it', 'Happens-before on lock/unlock fixes atomicity AND visibility', 'volatile: visibility only; atomics: CAS; ReentrantLock: advanced control', 'Latch/barrier/semaphore for coordination patterns', 'ConcurrentHashMap and BlockingQueue over hand-rolled sync'], commonMistakes: ['Using volatile for count++ - visibility without atomicity', 'Forgetting finally { lock.unlock() } with ReentrantLock'], interviewTips: ['Demonstrate the non-volatile flag infinite loop', 'Rank synchronized, volatile, atomic, Lock by capability'] },
  { topic: 'Lambda Expressions', title: 'Lambda Expressions and Functional Interfaces', content: 'A lambda is an inline implementation of a FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE - an interface with exactly one abstract method (SAM). (a, b) -> a + b implements BinaryOperator; () -> 42 implements Supplier. The @FunctionalInterface annotation enforces single-abstract-method at compile time. The core interfaces in java.util.function: Function<T,R> (apply), Predicate<T> (test -> boolean), Consumer<T> (accept, side effect), Supplier<T> (get, produce), plus primitive specialisations (IntPredicate, ToIntFunction) and two-arg BiFunction/BiPredicate - knowing this table cold is the baseline.\n\nMethod references are lambda shorthand: ClassName::staticMethod (Integer::parseInt), instance::method (System.out::println), ClassName::instanceMethod (String::toUpperCase - the first argument becomes the receiver), and constructor references (ArrayList::new). Variable capture: lambdas may use effectively final locals (never reassigned); instance fields are capturable freely - the restriction exists because captured locals are copied into the lambda instance. this inside a lambda refers to the ENCLOSING instance, unlike anonymous classes where this is the anonymous object.\n\nWhy they matter: lambdas replaced anonymous-class boilerplate, enabled behaviour parameterisation (pass comparators, predicates, callbacks as data), and underpin the Stream API and CompletableFuture composition. Under the hood lambdas are NOT anonymous classes - invokedynamic generates the implementation lazily, cheaper on startup. Interview framing: define SAM and lambda syntax, recite the four core functional interfaces, the method-reference forms, then capture rules and the this distinction.', keyPoints: ['Lambda = implementation of a single-abstract-method interface', 'Core: Function, Predicate, Consumer, Supplier (+Bi/primitive variants)', 'Method refs: static, bound, unbound, constructor', 'Captured locals must be effectively final', 'this in a lambda = enclosing instance, not the lambda'], commonMistakes: ['Assuming default methods block SAM-ness - they do not count', 'Mutating captured variables and hitting the compiler error'], interviewTips: ['Map a lambda to each of the four core interfaces', 'Explain why capture requires effectively final'] },
  { topic: 'Streams API', title: 'Streams API', content: 'A Stream is a declarative, lazy pipeline over a source: source -> intermediate ops -> terminal op. Intermediate operations (filter, map, flatMap, sorted, distinct, peek, limit, skip) return streams and do NOTHING until a terminal operation (forEach, collect, reduce, count, min/max, anyMatch, findFirst, toArray) pulls elements - laziness lets the engine fuse operations and short-circuit (findFirst on an infinite stream terminates). Streams are single-use: a second terminal op throws IllegalStateException; and they do not mutate the source - collect produces new results.\n\nflatMap flattens nested structures (list of lists -> list; splitting lines into words) and is the signature op to demonstrate. Collectors power the terminal side: Collectors.toList(), joining(), groupingBy (the GROUP BY equivalent, with downstream collectors for counting/mapping/averaging), partitioningBy (boolean split), counting(), toMap with a merge function for duplicate keys. Reductions: reduce(identity, accumulator, combiner) generalises sum/max/custom folds; primitive streams IntStream.range and mapToInt avoid boxing and give sum/average/statistics. Parallel streams (.parallelStream()) split via ForkJoin - worth it only for large CPU-bound sources over cheap-to-split structures (arrays, ArrayList), never for I/O or shared-state side effects.\n\nThe interview traps: stateful intermediate ops (sorted, distinct) limit parallel efficiency; peek is for debugging, not logic; side effects in forEach over shared state break parallel streams; group-by-and-count (Collectors.groupingBy(x, counting())) is THE most-asked live-coding exercise. Interview framing: pipeline anatomy + laziness, flatMap demo, the collector family, then parallel-stream caveats.', keyPoints: ['Lazy intermediates; nothing runs until a terminal op', 'Single-use; sources unmodified; results via collect/reduce', 'flatMap flattens nested streams - the signature demo', 'groupingBy/partitioningBy/toMap cover most reporting needs', 'Parallel streams: CPU-bound, splittable, stateless only'], commonMistakes: ['Reusing a consumed stream', 'Mutating shared state inside forEach on parallel streams'], interviewTips: ['Live-code word-frequency with groupingBy + counting', 'Explain laziness with an infinite-stream findFirst example'] },
  { topic: 'File I/O', title: 'File I/O in Java', content: 'Modern Java file I/O starts with java.nio.file: Path (Paths.get / Path.of) describes a location; Files is the utility class - Files.readString/writeString (one-liners, Java 11+), Files.readAllLines, Files.lines (lazy streaming, must close), Files.newBufferedReader/newBufferedWriter, copy/move/delete with StandardCopyOption, createTempFile, and walk() for recursive tree traversal with a stream of Paths. Always prefer NIO over the legacy java.io.File - better exceptions, symlinks, attributes, and watch services.\n\nByte vs character matters: InputStream/OutputStream move raw bytes (images, binary); Reader/Writer decode characters with a Charset - ALWAYS specify StandardCharsets.UTF_8 explicitly, because platform-default charsets broke code across machines for decades. Buffered variants (BufferedReader/Writer, BufferedInputStream/OutputStream) wrap raw streams with an in-memory buffer, converting system-call-per-byte into bulk transfers - the single biggest I/O performance lever; BufferedReader also gives readLine(). Object serialization (ObjectOutputStream + Serializable) is legacy - versioning pain and security issues; prefer JSON/protobuf for persistence.\n\nResource discipline: every stream must close - use try-with-resources (AutoCloseable) exclusively; closing a wrapper closes the underlying stream. Files.lines and Files.walk return lazy streams that HOLD an open handle - wrap them in try-with-resources or leak file descriptors. Interview framing: NIO.2 utility tour, byte-vs-character with explicit UTF-8, buffering as the performance story, then try-with-resources discipline.', keyPoints: ['NIO.2: Path + Files utilities replace java.io.File', 'Bytes (streams) vs chars (readers) - always pass an explicit Charset', 'Buffering converts per-byte syscalls into bulk I/O', 'try-with-resources closes wrappers and their underlying streams', 'Files.lines/walk are lazy and hold handles - close them'], commonMistakes: ['Relying on the platform default charset', 'Not closing Files.lines streams - descriptor leaks'], interviewTips: ['Read a UTF-8 file line by line in three lines of code', 'Explain why buffering dominates raw read() performance'] },
  { topic: 'Garbage Collection', title: 'Garbage Collection and JVM Memory', content: 'Garbage collection automatically reclaims objects no longer reachable. Reachability starts from GC ROOTS (thread stacks, static fields, JNI references): an object is live if a root can reach it through references - so the classic leak patterns are static collections that only grow, unclosed resources, listeners never deregistered, and ThreadLocals in pools. Reference strengths shape strategy: strong (default, never collected), soft (collected under memory pressure - caches), weak (collected at next GC - WeakHashMap keys), phantom (for cleanup scheduling). Finalize() is deprecated; use try-with-resources/Cleaner.\n\nJVM memory: heap split into YOUNG generation (Eden + two Survivor spaces) and OLD generation, plus Metaspace (class metadata, off-heap since Java 8) and thread stacks. New objects allocate in Eden; a minor GC fills survivors with still-live objects, aging them; objects surviving enough minor GCs promote to Old. Minor GCs are frequent and fast (copying collector over a small region); a major/full GC sweeps the whole old generation and pauses everything unless the collector is concurrent. The generational hypothesis - most objects die young - is the WHY behind the whole layout; quoting it earns credit.\n\nCollectors: Serial (single-threaded, small heaps), Parallel (throughput, default for batch), G1 (default since Java 9 - region-based, concurrent marking, predictable pause targets), ZGC and Shenandoah (sub-millisecond pauses for huge heaps). Tuning levers: -Xms/-Xmx heap size, -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis, -XX:+UseG1GC. Diagnose with jstat, jmap heap dumps, and GC logs. Interview framing: reachability from roots + leak patterns, generational layout with the hypothesis, then collector families and diagnosis tools.', keyPoints: ['GC roots define liveness: stacks, statics, JNI', 'Leak classics: static maps, listeners, unclosed resources, ThreadLocal', 'Young (Eden + survivors) vs Old; minor GC fast, major GC costly', 'Most objects die young - the generational hypothesis', 'G1 default (regions, pause targets); ZGC/Shenandoah sub-ms'], commonMistakes: ['Blaming GC for leaks - leaks are REACHABLE garbage', 'Calling System.gc() in production code'], interviewTips: ['Explain Eden-to-Old promotion step by step', 'Name two leak patterns and how to spot them in a heap dump'] },
];